We use the generating function
\[
\sum_{n=0}^\infty \binom{2n}{n}x^n=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-4x}}\quad (|x|<\tfrac14).
\]
Here \(x=\frac15\), so
\[
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\binom{2n}{n}\frac{1}{5^n}
=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-\frac{4}{5}}}
=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\frac{1}{5}}}
=\sqrt{5}.
\]

\boxed{\sqrt{5}}

Let \(B\) be the number of games Blaise wins out of 6. Since each game is a fair win/loss and independent, \(B\sim \text{Binomial}(6,\tfrac12)\). Then
\[
\mathbb P(B=3)=\binom{6}{3}\left(\tfrac12\right)^6=\frac{20}{64}=\frac{5}{16},
\]
matching the given probability.

“Blaise wins more than Pierre” means \(B>3\), i.e. \(B=4,5,6\). By symmetry,
\[
\mathbb P(B>3)=\mathbb P(B<3)=\frac{1-\mathbb P(B=3)}{2}
=\frac{1-\frac{5}{16}}{2}
=\frac{\frac{11}{16}}{2}
=\frac{11}{32}.
\]

\boxed{\frac{11}{32}}

